# Briefapp Atomic Flow — Cursor Skill

## Overview

The **Atomic-Agent Flow** is a fractal planning methodology where the **Complexity Multiplier C** defines the density of work decomposition. The higher C, the more granular and traceable the result.

## Step 0 — Choose Complexity Multiplier (C)

| C   | Typical Scenario                            | Backlogs | Sprints/Backlog | Tasks/Sprint | Subtasks/Task |
|-----|---------------------------------------------|----------|-----------------|--------------|---------------|
| 0.2 | Point fix / isolated bug                    | 1        | 1               | 1            | 1             |
| 0.5 | Small feature / simple improvement          | 3        | 1               | 2            | 2             |
| 1   | New module / medium initiative              | 5        | 2               | 3            | 4             |
| 2   | Complex feature / multiple domains          | 10       | 4               | 6            | 8             |
| 3   | Structural refactor / large epic            | 15       | 6               | 9            | 12            |

> **Formula:** Backlogs = 5×C | Sprints/Backlog = 2×C | Tasks/Sprint = 3×C | Subtasks/Task = 4×C

## Step 1 — CSV Planning

Before any MCP mass creation, draft the full plan in `briefapp_plan.csv`:
```csv
Type,ID,Title,Description,ParentID,Status
Box,box1,"Module Name","Description",,todo
Backlog,B1,"Functional Area","Description",box1,todo
Sprint,S1,"Sprint 1","",B1,todo
Task,T1,"Task 1.1","Description",S1,todo
Subtask,ST1,"Subtask 1.1.1","Do X",T1,todo
```

## Step 2 — 3-Level Branch Architecture

`develop` → `feature/{backlog_id}` → `task/{task_id}`

Subtasks DO NOT create branches and are worked directly on the task branch. Merge bubble-up on completion:
```bash
git checkout feature/{backlog_id} && git merge task/{task_id} --no-ff && git branch -d task/{task_id}
git checkout develop && git merge feature/{backlog_id} --no-ff && git branch -d feature/{backlog_id}
```

## Step 3 — TDD per Subtask (Red → Green → Refactor)

| Step | Action | Commit format |
|------|--------|---------------|
| 🔴 RED | Write test BEFORE implementation — must fail | `test: add failing test for <feature>` |
| 🟢 GREEN | Minimum implementation to pass — no over-engineering | `feat: implement <feature> to pass tests` |
| 🔵 REFACTOR | Clean code; all tests must keep passing | `refactor: clean up <feature>` |

**Minimum coverage: ≥ 80%**

## Step 4 — Commit Traceability Footer

Every commit MUST contain:
```
<type>: <short description>

Refs: backlog/<id> | sprint/<id> | task/<id>
```

## Step 5 — Status Sync

| Event | Tool | Status |
|-------|------|--------|
| Subtask created | `workitem_update` | `"todo"` |
| Starting implementation | `workitem_update` | `"in_progress"` |
| Subtask completed | `workitem_update` + commit | `"done"` |
| Blocked | `workitem_update` | `"blocked"` |

## Non-Negotiable Rules

- Never generate code without prior test — TDD is mandatory
- Never skip CSV checkpoint before MCP mass creation
- Never omit `Refs:` block in any commit
- Coverage < 80% = subtask not completed
- No silent mock/fallback in runtime
- Start next subtask only after committing and closing current one
